Both catalogs are complete. Bima's iPhone holds his share of the Line 1 check
(Workflow A) and his own Line 2 (Workflow B). In this tutorial, Bima sends his
catalog to Heru, and one merge on the iPad brings in both.

## 1. Export Bima's catalog and merge it

{% steps %}

1. On the iPhone, Bima opens the menu and selects `Export project`. Turn on
   `Light export` for a small `JSON.GZ` file without media (about 3 KB here),
   select `Export project`, and share the file with Heru.
2. On the iPad, Heru opens the menu, selects `Merge project`, and chooses the
   file with `Choose archive`. Check that `Project UUID` matches.
3. Select `Back up now`, tick the backup reminder, and select `Continue`.
4. Step through each section and select `Merge project` at the end.

{% /steps %}

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-export-project.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Export project screen on an iPhone with Light export on and the file name tujuh-teamwork-2014-nahpu-transfer.json.gz"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

## 2. Review each section

The merge shows how well the shared setup worked. Under `Personnel`, all three
people are `Matched` and none is new. Bima's record is marked `Conflict` only
because his cataloger number differs: `1` on the iPad and `5` on the iPhone
after five specimens. For each cataloger, keep the copy from the device they
catalog on—choose `Use imported` for Bima and keep `Keep current` for Heru.

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-merge-personnel.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Merge project Personnel step on an iPad with 0 new, 3 matched, and 0 needing attention; Bima Sampel's conflict action set to Use imported"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad" /%}

Under `Taxonomy`, every taxon is matched. Under `Sites` and `Events`, the two
workflows show side by side: the shared Line 1 records (Workflow A) are
`Matched`, so the team keeps one shared site catalog, while Bima's own `TJBS-L2`
site and event (Workflow B) are `New`. Keep `Keep current` for the matched
records. The `Conflict` labels here only mean that a matching record exists.

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-merge-sites.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Merge project Sites step on an iPad with 1 new, 2 matched, and 0 needing attention; TJHH-L1 and TJHH-P1 are listed as matches"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad" /%}

Under `Specimens`, Bima's five records are new, with nothing needing attention.

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-merge-specimens.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Merge project Specimens step on an iPad with 5 new, 0 matched, and no conflicts"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad" /%}

## 3. Check the merged records

Nothing is written until the final `Merge project`, and a failure rolls
everything back. Afterward, open `Data table` under `Tools` on the dashboard:
both catalogs now sit in one project, with three sites and three events.

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-data-table-merged.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Data table on an iPad listing ten specimen records: Heru Handika 445 to 449 (Muridae), Bima Sampel 1 to 4 (Soricidae), and Bima Sampel 5 (Maxomys hylomyoides)"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad" /%}

{% aside type="tip" %}
For conflicts and duplicate identifiers in larger merges, see
[Merge a project and resolve conflicts](/en/how-to-recipes/protect-and-collaborate/merge-project/).
{% /aside %}

